# Canonical host and caching policy for the zupu.

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ONE HOST, ONE COOKIE JAR.
#
# www.zupu.accme.my served the byte-identical app on a DIFFERENT cookie
# origin. The session cookie is host-only, so a sign-in on one host left the
# other signed out, and clicking the emailed link again could never fix it —
# the link lands on the apex, the bookmark returns to www, and the site says
# "signed out" forever with nothing visibly wrong. Reproduced with a real
# session cookie: www answered signedIn:false.
#
# Nobody had reached it yet (0 of 3,020 requests over ten days), which is the
# cheapest possible moment to close a door like this.
#
# The condition can only match a host beginning "www.", so the apex redirects
# to itself under no circumstances. %{REQUEST_URI} carries the path and
# mod_rewrite re-appends the query string, so deep links survive.
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
<IfModule mod_rewrite.c>
  RewriteEngine On
  RewriteCond %{HTTP_HOST} ^www\.(.+)$ [NC]
  RewriteRule ^ https://%1%{REQUEST_URI} [R=301,L]
</IfModule>

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CACHING.
#
# THE PROBLEM THIS SOLVES. The page loads js/app.js by a plain path and the
# server sent no Cache-Control at all, so a browser was free to decide for
# itself how long to keep it — and did. A reviewer reported that "mark as
# verified" did nothing; the button was fine, her browser was running a copy
# of app.js from before that capability existed, and a click fell through to a
# branch that silently does nothing. There is a proxy cache in front of this
# site too (x-proxy-cache-info), so a stale index.html can be served even when
# the file on disk is current.
#
# The shape of the fix is the ordinary one: the HTML is small and must always
# be fresh, because it is what names the version of everything else;
# deploy_frontend.sh stamps every local script and stylesheet with ?v=<deploy>,
# so those URLs change whenever their contents do and can be cached hard.
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
<IfModule mod_headers.c>
  # The entry point. no-store rather than no-cache: it also asks the proxy in
  # front not to hold a copy, which is what actually bit us.
  <FilesMatch "^index\.html$">
    Header set Cache-Control "no-store, must-revalidate"
    Header set Pragma "no-cache"
  </FilesMatch>

  # Versioned assets. Safe to keep for a year precisely because the URL changes
  # with every deploy — nothing here is ever served stale under its own name.
  <FilesMatch "\.(js|css|webp|png|jpg|jpeg|gif|svg|woff2?)$">
    Header set Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000"
  </FilesMatch>

  # NOTHING FOR .php, DELIBERATELY.
  #
  # This used to be `<FilesMatch "\.php$"> Header unset Cache-Control`,
  # explained as letting photo.php decide its own caching. It did the exact
  # opposite: `unset` strips the header PHP just sent, so json_out()'s
  # `private, no-store` and photo.php's `private, no-store` for a MEMBER's
  # photo both left this server with no cache directive at all — the one
  # distinction the rule existed to protect was the one it destroyed. Every
  # response was then at the mercy of whatever a shared cache chose to infer.
  #
  # It was never needed either: the asset rule above matches on extension, and
  # a .php file has never matched it. Removing the block is the whole fix.
  #
  # So: photo.php sends `public, max-age=3600` or `private, no-store`
  # depending on the tier, doc.php and every JSON endpoint send
  # `private, no-store`, and all of them now arrive intact.
</IfModule>
